---
layout: article
title: Track a website
description: Add Appwrite Analytics to a website or web app with one script tag or the Web SDK. Track pageviews, outbound links, scroll depth, engagement time, and file downloads from the browser.
---

The Appwrite Web SDK includes a `Tracking` helper that records visits from the browser. A call to `start()` tracks pageviews, outbound link clicks, scroll depth, and engagement time. File downloads and your own events each take one more line.

On a site without a build step, such as a static site or a CMS theme, the tracker script sets up `Tracking` from one script tag. In an app with a build step, install the SDK from npm instead.

# Before you start {% #before-you-start %}

You need an [analytics property](/docs/products/analytics/properties) and its property ID, which the Console shows next to the property name.

Your site also needs a Web app in the same project. Browsers send an `Origin` header with every event, and Appwrite accepts events from a browser only when that hostname belongs to one of the project's apps. Without one, every event fails with a `403` `general_unknown_origin` error.

1. In your project, open **Apps** and click **Add app**.
2. Choose **Web**.
3. Enter your site's hostname, for example `example.com`, and finish the wizard.

Add one app for each hostname your site runs on, including `localhost` while you develop.

# Add the script tag {% #script-tag %}

Add the tracker script to the `<head>` of every page. It loads the Web SDK, creates a `Tracking` instance, and calls `start()`, so it needs no other code.

```html
<script defer src="https://sdk.appwrite.io/web/29.2.0/tracker.js"
        data-endpoint="https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1"
        data-project="<PROJECT_ID>"
        data-property="<PROPERTY_ID>"></script>
```

The script reads its settings from the `data-` attributes of its own tag.

| Attribute | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `data-endpoint` | Your project's API endpoint, as a full URL. A path such as `/relay` doesn't work. |
| `data-project` | Your project ID. |
| `data-property` | Your property ID. |
| `data-track-downloads` | Optional. `true` turns on [download tracking](#downloads). |
| `data-respect-dnt` | Optional. `false` also tracks visitors who have [Do Not Track](#do-not-track) turned on. |

The script sends the same [automatic events](#automatic-events) as `start()`. Open your site, then open the property in the Console. The visit appears on the dashboard within a few seconds.

## Use the tracker from your code {% #script-tag-code %}

The script stores its `Tracking` instance on `window.__appwriteTracker`, so you can send [custom events](/docs/products/analytics/custom-events) and turn helpers on and off. A deferred script runs after the inline scripts of the page, so wait for `DOMContentLoaded` before you use the tracker while the page loads.

```html
<script>
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
        window.__appwriteTracker.track('signup_completed', {
            props: { plan: 'pro' },
        });
    });
</script>
```

Code that runs later, such as a click handler, can use `window.__appwriteTracker` directly.

The script tag tracks one property. To also send events to a second property, call `Appwrite.startTracking()`. It takes `endpoint`, `project`, and `property`, and the optional `respectDoNotTrack` and `trackDownloads`, and returns the started `Tracking` instance.

```html
<script>
    document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
        Appwrite.startTracking({
            endpoint: 'https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1',
            project: '<PROJECT_ID>',
            property: '<SECOND_PROPERTY_ID>',
        });
    });
</script>
```

## Check the file's integrity {% #script-tag-integrity %}

To have the browser check that the file is unchanged, add its hash as the `integrity` attribute, together with `crossorigin="anonymous"`. Each release lists the hash of every file in `integrity.json`, such as `https://sdk.appwrite.io/web/29.2.0/integrity.json`.

```html
<script defer src="https://sdk.appwrite.io/web/29.2.0/tracker.js"
        integrity="sha384-M1DbhtkbnViz6JLD9On/0rc2MkZAXRrfr9I25PJKwZY5uUxON3dukLPp73l0fodH"
        crossorigin="anonymous"
        data-endpoint="https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1"
        data-project="<PROJECT_ID>"
        data-property="<PROPERTY_ID>"></script>
```

# Install the SDK {% #install %}

To create `Tracking` in your own code, install the Web SDK from npm.

```sh
npm install appwrite
```

# Start tracking {% #start-tracking %}

Create a `Tracking` instance with the `Analytics` service and your property ID, then call `start()`.

```client-web
import { Client, Analytics, Tracking } from 'appwrite';

const client = new Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>');

const tracking = new Tracking(new Analytics(client), '<PROPERTY_ID>');

tracking.start();
```

Create one `Tracking` instance per page load and start it once. In a framework that renders on the server, run this code in the browser only, for example in a component that mounts once at the root of your app.

Open your site, then open the property in the Console. The visit appears on the dashboard within a few seconds.

# Automatic events {% #automatic-events %}

`start()` turns on four helpers. Each one sends events with a fixed name.

| Event | When it's sent | Extra data |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `pageview` | When the page loads, and on every route change made through the History API (`pushState`, `replaceState`, back and forward) or a hash change | The page URL, the referrer, and the viewport width |
| `outbound_link` | When a visitor clicks a link to another site | `hostname` and `href` props |
| `scroll_depth` | When the visitor changes route or leaves the page, if they scrolled it | The deepest point scrolled on that page, from `0` to `100` percent |
| `engagement_time` | When the tab is hidden, when the visitor changes route, and when the page unloads | The seconds the page was visible since the previous `engagement_time` event |

Because pageviews follow the History API, single-page apps built with React Router, Next.js, Vue Router, SvelteKit, and similar routers are tracked without extra code.

A link counts as outbound when its hostname is on a different domain from the current page. Links between subdomains of the same domain, such as `example.com` and `docs.example.com`, are not outbound.

# Track file downloads {% #downloads %}

Download tracking is off by default, because the file types worth counting depend on your site. Turn it on after `start()`. With the script tag, add `data-track-downloads="true"` instead.

```client-web
tracking.enableAutoDownloadTracking();
```

When a visitor clicks a link to a file with a tracked extension, `Tracking` sends a `file_download` event with `filename`, `extension`, and `href` props. The default extensions are `pdf`, `zip`, `rar`, `7z`, `tar`, `gz`, `csv`, `tsv`, `xls`, `xlsx`, `doc`, `docx`, `ppt`, `pptx`, `dmg`, `exe`, `msi`, `pkg`, `deb`, `rpm`, `apk`, `mp3`, `wav`, `flac`, `mp4`, `mov`, `avi`, `mkv`, and `webm`.

Pass `extensions` to track your own list instead. With the script tag, leave `data-track-downloads` out and call `enableAutoDownloadTracking()` on `window.__appwriteTracker`.

```client-web
tracking.enableAutoDownloadTracking({
    extensions: ['pdf', 'epub', 'zip'],
});
```

# Choose what to track {% #choose-helpers %}

Instead of `start()`, you can turn on each helper on its own. This is also how you pass options to the outbound link helper. With `includeSubdomains: true`, a link to any other hostname counts as outbound, including subdomains of your own domain.

```client-web
const tracking = new Tracking(new Analytics(client), '<PROPERTY_ID>');

tracking.enableAutoPageviews();
tracking.enableAutoOutboundTracking({ includeSubdomains: true });
tracking.enableAutoEngagementTime();
```

Each helper has a method to turn it on and one to turn it off:

- **Pageviews**: `enableAutoPageviews()` and `disableAutoPageviews()`
- **Outbound links**: `enableAutoOutboundTracking()` and `disableAutoOutboundTracking()`
- **Scroll depth**: `enableAutoScrollDepth()` and `disableAutoScrollDepth()`
- **Engagement time**: `enableAutoEngagementTime()` and `disableAutoEngagementTime()`
- **File downloads**: `enableAutoDownloadTracking()` and `disableAutoDownloadTracking()`

Calling a turn-on method twice has no effect. To change a helper's options, turn it off first.

To send your own events, such as a signup or a purchase, see [Track custom events](/docs/products/analytics/custom-events).

# Do Not Track {% #do-not-track %}

By default, `Tracking` sends nothing for visitors whose browser has Do Not Track turned on. It checks the Do Not Track setting only, not Global Privacy Control. To count these visitors as well, set `respectDoNotTrack` to `false`. With the script tag, add `data-respect-dnt="false"` instead.

```client-web
const tracking = new Tracking(new Analytics(client), '<PROPERTY_ID>', {
    respectDoNotTrack: false,
});
```

For what Appwrite stores about each visitor, see [Privacy](/docs/products/analytics/privacy).

# Troubleshooting {% #troubleshooting %}

`Tracking` never throws and never interrupts your page. When an event fails, it logs the warning `Tracking: failed to send event` to the browser console, with the error.

- **`403` with `general_unknown_origin`**: The page's hostname isn't an app in your project. Add it under **Apps**.
- **`401` with `general_access_forbidden`**: The property limits which origins may send events, and this page's origin isn't on the list. See [Properties](/docs/products/analytics/properties).
- **`404` with `analytics_property_not_found`**: The property ID is wrong, or the property is turned off.
- **Nothing in the network panel**: Do Not Track is on in your browser.
- **Requests marked as blocked in the network panel**: an extension, such as an ad blocker, blocks them. See [Serve tracking from your domain](/docs/products/analytics/own-domain).

The tracker script logs its own warnings when it can't start:

- **`Tracker: script tag is missing data-property`**: the tag is missing a required attribute. The warning names each missing one.
- **`Tracker: failed to start`** with `Invalid endpoint URL`: `data-endpoint` isn't a full URL, such as `https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1`.
- **`Tracker: already started, ignoring this script tag`**: the page has more than one tracker tag, and only the first one runs. To track a second property, use [`Appwrite.startTracking()`](#script-tag-code).
